{
 "version": "vol11",
 "label": "Modeled",
 "unit": "fresh-set equivalents per set",
 "anchor": "1.0 = one fresh hard set of machine-chest-press (accessory, machine compound) at its planned 2 RIR: type_base 1.0 x axial 1.0 x effort 1.0",
 "formula": "fatigue_per_set = recovery.params.type_base[type] x axial(exercise) (axial classes: high 1.3 / free_compound 1.1 / machine_compound 1.0 / isolation 0.9). Already on the anchor scale, so the rescale factor is 1.0.",
 "per_muscle": "muscle fatigue per set = rating[m] x fatigue_per_set x effort[RIR + role offset] (effort: RIR 0 1.2, 1 1.1, 2 1.0, 3 0.9, 4+ 0.8; role offsets as in volume-model.json credit). Muscles rated < 0.25 get 0.",
 "manual_adjustments": [],
 "manual_adjustments_note": "None. Every value comes from type x axial; no lift was hand-tuned.",
 "basis": "Heavy axial compounds (heavy squat/hinge lists, fatigue rating 1.5, good mornings) > 1, isolation < 1. Classes come from existing exercises.json data (type, equipment, fatigue).",
 "nurd": {
  "short": "Lift Board model; effort factors anchored to Moran-Navarro 2017 and Refalo 2024",
  "year": 2026,
  "type": "model",
  "label": "Modeled",
  "note": "Modeled. type_base and axial classes are planning factors from existing exercise data; the RIR effort factor follows studies showing slower recovery after sets to failure (Moran-Navarro et al. 2017, Eur J Appl Physiol) and similar hypertrophy with less fatigue short of failure (Refalo et al. 2024). No study gives per-exercise fatigue multipliers."
 },
